    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Toyota Puts Snow Tracks on Five SUVs and Trucks, Unloads Them at SEMA 2026 as the Snowhunter Bunch\nEHang Will Send a Fleet of 25 Autonomous eVTOLs to Thailand\n7 photos\nPhoto: EHang\nEHang is one of the global leaders in pilotless eVTOL (electric vertical takeoff and landing) technology, having reached the finish line in terms of regulatory approvals fasters than anyone else. With its autonomous eVTOLs already operating domestically, EHang is now focusing on expanding its portfolio across Southeast Asia.\nThailand is becoming one of the most important targets for EHang, the Chinese AAM (Advanced Air Mobility) company that has fully certified its flagship, the pilotless EH216-S. EHang announced plans to deploy an entire fleet of 25 EH216-S aircraft to this country, not as part of some purchase agreement with a private operator, but for the company’s own expansion plan.\nThe deployment is part of a wider strategy to establish a regional hub in Thailand, intended to become the central hub for EHang’s expansion across Southeast Asia and other emerging markets. By sending those 25 aircraft to the area, EHang will have these assets and all the resources related to them at hand for all of its future regional projects.\nThis is meant to become EHang’s “regional fleet” that will be used across a series of operations, from market-specific flight validation to operational preparation in multiple countries in the region – and there are many such projects already planned, as part of the company’s Global Fast Track Program and AAM sandbox initiatives.\nIn fact, EHang is preparing for a very busy month of November on the regional side. Validation flights are scheduled to take place at Port City Colombo in Sri Lanka, in Hanoi, Vietnam, and in Putrajaya, Malaysia.\nBefore the end of this year, EHang will round up its Southeast Asia projects with several demonstration flights in Thailand, during an important event, the International Civil Aviation Organization (“ICAO”) AAM Symposium, taking place in Bangkok, in December 2026.\nThe 25 EH216-S eVTOLs that are about to be deployed in Thailand will join the already existing GD4.0 formation drones that have been previously sent there for EHang’s drone light show business. More of these drone for commercial light shows will also be sent in the near future, with plans to reach a stock of up to 10,000 units.\nThe Chinese EH216-S was the first eVTOL of its kind (pilotless, passenger-carrying) in the world to secure type certificate (TC), production certificate (PC) and standard airworthiness certificate (AC), all issued by the Civil Aviation Administration of China (CAAC). This allows full commercial operations in China.\nEHang is also developing and preparing to launch a long-range version, the VT35, which will expand potential applications to inter-city mobility. Meanwhile, the versatile EH216-S supports a wide range of applications across short-distance passenger mobility, emergency response, and aerial logistics.\nEHang is also one of the most prolific AAM companies when it comes to international expansion, with a strong presence in emerging markets like Kazakhstan and Turkey.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nautonomous eVTOL Ehang Chinese eVTOL EH216-S evtol\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT What If Stellantis Allowed SRT To Bring Back the GTX Nameplate Based on a 1970s Plymouth – Rendering",
